  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 AM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nFriday was absolutely pleasant for late November with highs well \ninto the 60s. We'll play this track again today with a 576dm ridge \nstill in place. These heights are a touch lower that 24 hours prior \nwhile 850mb temps are about 2C warmer and our morning lows are \nwarmer as well, providing a better spring board for the \nafternoon. This all to say afternoon highs will be comparable to\nFriday, once again challenging records.\n\nBack to reality next week in the wake of a cold front that moves \nthrough later Sunday. Breezy and mild ahead of the front Sunday \nmorning with rain arriving to northwest Indiana during the early \nafternoon, gradually tracking east thereafter. Rain amounts \nwill be light, at less than 0.10\", as the best upper-level \nforcing lifts north and the precipitation shield along the front\nbattles preexisting high pressure over the Appalachians.\n\nCanadian high pressure spills over the Northern Plains Sunday \nnight through Monday with a secondary shortwave noted Monday \nnight. This shortwave still threatens to provide a brief period \nof lake effect to the eastern Great Lakes but duration, if at \nall, appears brief. High pressure quickly arriving limits fetch\nand equilibrium levels, as guidance continues to offer a \nvariety of coverage solutions for any POPs. The official forecast\nremains dry at this time.\n\nAn eastern US trough near Thanksgiving remains somewhat murky \nlocally, yet most signs favor any precipitation to be east of the \nIWX coverage area. Using Grand Ensemble clusters, a GEFS/ENS/GEPS \nsplit yields only a 35% chance of precipitation >0.01\", while a 4th \ncluster weighted toward the GEFS yields a 40% chance. 20-30% POPs \nremain the forecast, primarily along and east of I-69 corridor. \n\nBroad upper-level troughing remains in place through the end of the \nweek. An upstream ridge is noted thereafter which may promote a\nmoderating trend once more as the calendar turns to December.",
